WEBVTT

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.600
Energy drains are the conditions that make useful work more expensive than it needs to be.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.720
Unclear priorities force repeated decisions about what matters.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:10.720
Open loops keep unresolved tasks, questions, commitments,

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.285
or decisions active in the background.

00:00:12.285 --> 00:00:14.345
Multitasking fragments attention.

00:00:14.345 --> 00:00:16.140
Constant availability prevents depth.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.280
Excessive meetings consume not only time, but also preparation, social energy,

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.000
and recovery.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.960
Unresolved conflict is another drain.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.500
Even when people avoid talking about it, conflict can absorb emotional energy,

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.410
distort communication, and slow decisions.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.090
Cluttered environments can create friction when tools, files, information,

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:33.615
or next steps are hard to find.

00:00:33.615 --> 00:00:36.595
Poor boundaries turn every request into a potential interruption.

00:00:36.595 --> 00:00:40.000
Rework drains energy because people have to pay again for work that could have been

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.040
clarified earlier.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.600
Hidden emotional labor is often invisible in plans.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.900
It includes smoothing tension, remembering interpersonal context,

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.580
supporting others through uncertainty, absorbing complaints,

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.480
or managing the emotional tone of a group.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.280
This work can be valuable, but it still consumes capacity.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:55.470
When it is not acknowledged,

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:59.315
people may feel depleted without being able to point to a visible task that explains it.

00:00:59.315 --> 00:01:01.145
Small drains compound across a week.

00:01:01.145 --> 00:01:04.640
A few extra status meetings, a cluttered task list, unclear ownership,

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.925
and constant message checking may not look dramatic in isolation.

00:01:07.925 --> 00:01:10.955
Together, they can consume the capacity needed for high-quality work.

00:01:10.955 --> 00:01:13.720
Before assuming a motivation problem, look for energy leaks.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.030
The practical question is what can be clarified, closed, batched, delegated, automated,

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.210
simplified, or protected.
